var insights = [
  { slug: "non-human-identity-governance", title: "What is Non Human Identity and how to govern it", description: "Identify, own and control service accounts, workload identities, API credentials and other machine access.", services: ["identity", "cybersecurity"], sections: [
    { title: "Identity is not limited to people", paragraphs: ["Non Human Identity, or NHI, describes the identities used by software, workloads and devices to authenticate and access resources. Examples include service accounts, cloud workload identities, automation accounts and device identities. API keys, certificates and tokens are credentials associated with those identities, not necessarily separate identities themselves.", "Machine access can continue after its original creator leaves or its application changes. Governance connects each identity to an accountable owner, a purpose and an explicit access boundary."] },
    { title: "Build an inventory with ownership", paragraphs: ["Start with identity providers, cloud accounts, secret stores, deployment systems and application configurations. Record the identity, the workload using it, its owner, the resources it can reach and how its credentials are issued. Do not copy secret values into the inventory.", "An owner should be a responsible team or role with a maintained escalation path. Document dependencies before disabling an account. A credential used by an unattended integration may have no obvious interactive sign in history."] },
    { title: "Prefer short lived, workload bound access", paragraphs: ["Where supported, use workload federation or platform managed identities instead of distributing persistent secrets. Limit permissions to the operations and resources a workload needs. Separate production access from development and test access.", "When long lived credentials are necessary, store them in an appropriate secret management system, restrict retrieval and define a tested rotation procedure. Rotation needs coordinated deployment and rollback, not simply a calendar reminder."] },
    { title: "Manage the full lifecycle", paragraphs: ["Issue access through a reviewed process. Observe how identities are used, review permissions and investigate unexpected changes. Include identity retirement in application decommissioning and supplier offboarding.", "Begin with one bounded environment. Assign owners, reduce excess access and test credential replacement before expanding the program. NHI governance complements privileged access management and identity governance; it does not replace application security or workload isolation."] }
  ] },
  { slug: "pam-vs-pim", title: "PAM vs PIM explained", description: "Understand how privileged access management and privileged identity management address different aspects of elevated access.", services: ["identity", "cybersecurity"], sections: [
    { title: "Two related ideas", paragraphs: ["Privileged Access Management, or PAM, governs access to powerful accounts, credentials and sessions. Privileged Identity Management, or PIM, often focuses on the lifecycle and activation of elevated roles assigned to identities. Product vendors use these terms differently, so compare capabilities rather than relying on a label.", "PAM can include credential vaulting, session brokering, session recording and controlled account use. PIM can include eligible role assignments, approval, time limited activation and reviews. A platform may offer both."] },
    { title: "Match controls to the access path", paragraphs: ["An administrator connecting to a legacy server using a shared credential may need a vault and a brokered session. A cloud operator assuming a named administrative role may need time limited activation, strong authentication and role assignment controls.", "Both situations need an accountable identity and audit evidence. A vault alone does not remove excessive privileges. Time limited activation alone does not control every credential or session in an environment."] },
    { title: "Design exceptions deliberately", paragraphs: ["Emergency access should remain usable during an identity service outage. Define who can use it, how credentials are protected, how use is detected and how access is reviewed afterwards. Test the process without leaving routine access permanently elevated.", "Service accounts and automation need a separate design because they cannot complete a human approval prompt. Limit their permissions, control their credentials and assign an owner."] },
    { title: "Evaluate the operational fit", paragraphs: ["Map privileged workflows before choosing tools. Check system coverage, identity integration, approval responsibilities, evidence retention and recovery behavior. Pilot with real administrative tasks and include failure scenarios.", "Use PAM and PIM as complementary controls within a wider access program. The desired outcome is understandable, limited and reviewable elevated access, not merely another product deployment."] }
  ] },
  { slug: "iam-vs-ciam", title: "IAM vs CIAM", description: "Compare workforce identity and customer identity requirements without confusing audiences or trust boundaries.", services: ["identity", "software-engineering"], sections: [
    { title: "Different audiences, shared foundations", paragraphs: ["Identity and Access Management, or IAM, is the broad discipline of managing identities and access. Workforce IAM focuses on employees, contractors and other organizational users. Customer Identity and Access Management, or CIAM, applies these foundations to users of customer facing products and services.", "Both involve authentication, authorization and lifecycle management. They differ in how identities are established, how accounts are supported and what users expect from the experience."] },
    { title: "Workforce access follows organizational responsibilities", paragraphs: ["Workforce identities usually connect to an employment or contractor relationship. Access often follows a role, team or location and changes through joiner, mover and leaver processes.", "Workforce controls may include federation, multifactor authentication, device requirements and privileged access. The organization needs a reliable source for lifecycle changes and clear responsibility for approvals."] },
    { title: "Customer identity follows the product relationship", paragraphs: ["CIAM may support self service registration, account recovery, customer consent and multiple organizations using the same application. The identity journey is part of the product and must be accessible as well as secure.", "Collect only attributes needed for the service. Separate identity verification from authentication and define when stronger proof is required. A successful sign in does not mean a user is authorized to access every tenant or record."] },
    { title: "Keep authorization and recovery explicit", paragraphs: ["An identity provider authenticates the user and issues information about that identity. The application must still enforce resource and tenant permissions on the server. Never rely only on what the browser chooses to display.", "Choose workforce and customer identity patterns based on lifecycle, privacy, recovery and integration requirements. Some organizations use separate identity systems for these audiences; others share infrastructure while preserving distinct policy and data boundaries."] }
  ] },
  { slug: "practical-zero-trust-roadmap", title: "A practical Zero Trust roadmap", description: "Develop a staged approach to identity, device, resource and network controls based on actual business workflows.", services: ["cybersecurity", "identity", "cloud"], sections: [
    { title: "Start with resources, not a product", paragraphs: ["Zero Trust is an approach to access decisions that does not grant implicit trust based only on network location or ownership. Access decisions consider the requesting identity, the resource, policy and available context. It is an architecture and operating discipline, not a single tool.", "Identify the systems and data that matter, who needs them and how access works today. Record dependencies and availability needs before changing enforcement."] },
    { title: "Establish reliable identity and device signals", paragraphs: ["Improve account lifecycle management, authentication and privileged access. Assess device posture signals and their limitations. A signal that is missing, stale or unreliable should have an explicit policy treatment.", "Map machine identities as well as human users. Define least privilege access to specific resources and make policy ownership clear. Avoid treating a successful multifactor challenge as permission for unrestricted access."] },
    { title: "Pilot enforcement around one workflow", paragraphs: ["Choose a bounded application or administrative workflow. Define the policy, test it in an observation mode where possible and verify that authorized users can complete their tasks. Include suppliers, remote users and recovery scenarios.", "Segment access paths where appropriate and reduce unnecessary reachability. Network controls complement identity and application authorization; they cannot compensate for a missing permission check in an application."] },
    { title: "Operate and improve", paragraphs: ["Collect evidence for policy decisions and investigate unexpected access or denials. Tune policies with the teams who use and support the systems. Review exception expiry and emergency access procedures.", "Expand incrementally based on observed results. Measure whether access is understandable, limited and supportable using objectives appropriate to your environment. Zero Trust does not promise that incidents cannot occur."] }
  ] },
  { slug: "on-premises-hybrid-multi-cloud", title: "Choosing between on premises, hybrid and multi cloud", description: "Compare hosting models through data location, operations, integration, resilience and portability requirements.", services: ["cloud", "advisory", "managed-services"], sections: [
    { title: "Define the models clearly", paragraphs: ["On premises systems run in infrastructure operated in an organization controlled facility or a contracted data center arrangement. Hybrid environments combine on premises or private infrastructure with public cloud services. Multi cloud uses services from more than one cloud provider and may also be part of a hybrid environment.", "These labels do not determine security, resilience or cost by themselves. Those outcomes depend on architecture, contracts, operational practices and the services selected."] },
    { title: "Begin with non negotiable constraints", paragraphs: ["Document data residency, legal obligations, integration dependencies, latency sensitivity and recovery requirements. Check the location of backups, support access and service metadata as well as primary application data.", "Separate legal requirements from preferences. Confirm how a proposed service handles data and operational access rather than assuming that a regional deployment resolves every residency issue."] },
    { title: "Compare the operational responsibilities", paragraphs: ["On premises infrastructure requires capacity planning, hardware lifecycle management and physical operations. Cloud services transfer some responsibilities to the provider while leaving identity, configuration, data protection and application controls with the customer.", "Hybrid environments need reliable connectivity and clear ownership across boundaries. Multi cloud can offer provider choice, but it also introduces differences in identity, networking, tooling and incident handling. A second provider does not automatically create a working recovery plan."] },
    { title: "Choose per workload and test the transition", paragraphs: ["Evaluate workloads against their constraints and the team\u2019s operating capacity. Include connectivity, data transfer, licenses, support and exit planning in the comparison. Favor a manageable design over a topology chosen only for flexibility.", "Before migration, test backup restoration, identity dependencies and rollback. Document what must remain available during an outage and who makes the decision to recover or move a workload."] }
  ] },
  { slug: "identity-governance-basics", title: "Identity governance (IGA) basics", description: "Learn the foundations of identity lifecycle, access requests, reviews and separation of duties.", services: ["identity", "cybersecurity"], sections: [
    { title: "Connect access to a business reason", paragraphs: ["Identity Governance and Administration, or IGA, helps an organization understand who has access, why it was granted and whether it remains appropriate. It commonly combines identity lifecycle management, access requests, provisioning and access reviews.", "IGA complements authentication and privileged access controls. A strong sign in does not prove that the account should still have its permissions."] },
    { title: "Make lifecycle changes dependable", paragraphs: ["Define authoritative identity sources and ownership of attributes. Establish joiner, mover and leaver processes with clear responsibilities. A role change may require removal of old access as well as addition of new access.", "Connectors automate some changes, but applications vary in what they support. Document manual tasks and reconcile actual application access with the intended state. Include contractors and machine identities where the chosen governance scope supports them."] },
    { title: "Make requests and reviews understandable", paragraphs: ["Access requests should describe the entitlement, business purpose, approver and any expiry. Reviewers need enough context to make a decision, including what the access permits and whether it is still needed.", "Define separation of duties rules where incompatible permissions would create risk. Exceptions need an owner, a reason and review. Avoid replacing thoughtful approvals with a large, unexplained checklist."] },
    { title: "Start with a bounded governance scope", paragraphs: ["Select applications with known owners and meaningful access data. Clean identity records, establish a consistent lifecycle and test provisioning and removal. Expand only when the process can be operated reliably.", "Maintain evidence of decisions and verify that removals actually reach target systems. Governance is an ongoing organizational process supported by technology, not a one time certification campaign."] }
  ] }
];
